// Regression case for commit fc46e9db: a `\u{...}` escape above the Unicode
// maximum (0x10FFFF) must be a parse error. Cedar rejects `"\u{110000}"` with
// `the input \u{110000} is not a valid escape`.
//
// This is the escape the pre-fix decode_string EXPLICITLY tried to handle:
// `u32::from_str_radix("110000", 16)` succeeds, then `char::from_u32` returns
// None for a non-scalar value, and the code's `None => pass through` branch
// emitted `\u{110000}` verbatim — accepted and validated cleanly instead of
// rejected. The clearest illustration of the commit's claim that the hand-
// rolled decoder "diverged from" Cedar: it recognized the shape and still got
// the answer wrong. (Lone surrogates like `\u{D800}` take the same buggy path.)
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when { context.input.document == "\u{110000}" };
